WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.399 --> 00:00:11.424
As long ago as 1865, French writer Jules Verne told a tale of men journeying to the moon.

2
00:00:12.465 --> 00:00:17.828
In this story, From the Earth to the Moon, three men blast off from Florida in an aluminum

3
00:00:17.869 --> 00:00:23.032
capsule. They land on the moon and return to Earth by splashing down in the Pacific Ocean.

4
00:00:23.993 --> 00:00:28.236
Over one hundred years later, Verne's fiction became reality.

5
00:00:29.136 --> 00:00:35.299
In July 1969, three men in a spacecraft launched from Florida and landed on the moon in a

6
00:00:35.359 --> 00:00:36.919
mission called Apollo 11.

7
00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:40.941
When they returned, they splashed down in the Pacific Ocean.

8
00:00:41.862 --> 00:00:48.424
The big difference between these excursions is that the Apollo astronauts traveled in a rocket, but Verne's fictional

9
00:00:48.484 --> 00:00:51.866
travelers made the trip in a capsule shot from a cannon.

10
00:00:53.146 --> 00:00:59.010
Verne's story and others like it are examples of a prophetic genre of literature called science fiction.

11
00:00:59.930 --> 00:01:06.776
The work of its writers has often predicted future technologies and even influenced scientists making new inventions.

12
00:01:07.697 --> 00:01:13.842
Even though most science fiction is never realized, the best sci-fi, as it is also known, does have

13
00:01:13.882 --> 00:01:14.822
a way of coming true.

14
00:01:15.903 --> 00:01:18.465
In short, science fiction is about change.

15
00:01:19.566 --> 00:01:24.010
It envisions how the world will look down the road and describes how we might live with new

16
00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:31.516
technologies. Before the modern age, full of inventions and scientific discoveries, most people were born into a world

17
00:01:31.536 --> 00:01:35.059
that looked pretty much the same as the one in which their parents were raised.

18
00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:42.666
Interestingly enough, very few writers before the contemporary period produced anything that could be called sci-fi.

19
00:01:43.827 --> 00:01:46.149
That all ended in the nineteenth century.

20
00:01:47.370 --> 00:01:53.956
In 1818, Mary Shelley published Frankenstein, one of the first major works of science fiction.

21
00:01:54.876 --> 00:01:59.998
In this story, a scientist assembles a creature from human body parts and brings it to life with

22
00:02:00.058 --> 00:02:03.180
electricity. Even today, the tale of Dr.

23
00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:07.362
Frankenstein and his monster is one of the best known science-fiction stories.

24
00:02:08.262 --> 00:02:14.405
Shelley's monster is still fictional, but Mary Shelley was right that electricity could be put to medical use.

25
00:02:15.385 --> 00:02:21.728
Today, doctors use electrical impulses to help people with amputations move their artificial legs and arms.

26
00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:27.663
Almost one hundred years after Frankenstein was published, British author H.

27
00:02:27.743 --> 00:02:31.166
G. Wells produced another major work that foretold the future.

28
00:02:32.086 --> 00:02:38.791
In his 1914 story The World Set Free, he wrote of atomic bombs well before they were created.

29
00:02:39.772 --> 00:02:43.895
Although his bombs worked very differently from the atomic bombs used by the U.S.

30
00:02:43.955 --> 00:02:48.938
in World War II, Wells accurately described their incredibly destructive power.

31
00:02:49.799 --> 00:02:56.047
Wells is also renowned for his 1898 novel The War of the Worlds, about an invasion from outer

32
00:02:56.107 --> 00:03:01.690
space. Thankfully, this plot has not come true yet—and likely never will.

33
00:03:02.530 --> 00:03:08.113
But people have become so fascinated with the idea of alien invaders from space that Wells' story has

34
00:03:08.153 --> 00:03:09.554
been retold many times.

35
00:03:11.115 --> 00:03:16.318
It has been adapted as a radio drama and three times as a movie, most recently as a

36
00:03:16.378 --> 00:03:21.938
Hollywood blockbuster by the same title, starring Tom Cruise and directed by Steven Spielberg.

37
00:03:23.239 --> 00:03:27.122
Wells was writing in the frightening time before the outbreak of World War I.

38
00:03:27.983 --> 00:03:30.985
The political atmosphere in Europe at this time was quite ominous.

39
00:03:32.126 --> 00:03:37.651
Nationalism was on the rise, with European nations racing to produce more and more armaments.

40
00:03:38.491 --> 00:03:39.572
War seemed inevitable.

41
00:03:40.553 --> 00:03:43.736
As a result, much of Wells' work tells of a depressing future.

42
00:03:44.857 --> 00:03:48.500
The work of the Czech writer Karel Čapek was also quite pessimistic.

43
00:03:49.780 --> 00:03:57.224
His play, R.U.R., which stands for Rossem's Universal Robots, introduced the world to a word that has become

44
00:03:57.264 --> 00:04:02.407
not only a common theme in science fiction, but also a household term: "robot."

45
00:04:03.247 --> 00:04:05.749
The word comes from the Czech word for slave.

46
00:04:06.649 --> 00:04:09.811
In Čapek's play, the robots are meant to free people from work.

47
00:04:11.072 --> 00:04:13.313
Instead, they end up taking over the world.

48
00:04:14.613 --> 00:04:19.937
The famous science-fiction writer Isaac Asimov further popularized the idea of robots.

49
00:04:20.918 --> 00:04:28.164
Asimov's stories, first published mainly in magazines in the 1930s and 1940s, continue to attract millions of readers.

50
00:04:29.164 --> 00:04:34.109
In them, he gives his robots human form, a notion that has been imitated in hundreds of films

51
00:04:34.249 --> 00:04:34.809
and novels.

52
00:04:35.610 --> 00:04:43.416
This type of character has even inspired a Japanese scientist, Hiroshi Ishiguro, to create androids so lifelike that

53
00:04:43.456 --> 00:04:48.784
they can flutter their eyelids, move their arms and legs like a real person, and even appear to

54
00:04:48.844 --> 00:04:54.066
breathe! Another popular and prophetic science-fiction writer from that era is Arthur C.

55
00:04:54.146 --> 00:05:02.088
Clarke. In his 1945 novel Fountains of Paradise, Clarke was the first to propose the geostationary satellite.

56
00:05:02.888 --> 00:05:08.590
To be in geostationary orbit, a satellite must circle the earth at exactly the same speed as the

57
00:05:08.670 --> 00:05:09.490
earth rotates.

58
00:05:10.390 --> 00:05:15.392
As a result, the satellite appears to hover in a fixed position above the earth's surface.

59
00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:19.361
Clarke even worked out the necessary math for such a satellite.

60
00:05:20.082 --> 00:05:27.825
Almost twenty years later, on August 19, 1964, a satellite was launched into geostationary orbit for the first

61
00:05:27.885 --> 00:05:34.888
time. Many believe that the most influential work of science fiction from the past forty years is Star

62
00:05:34.928 --> 00:05:41.530
Trek. Star Trek was originally a television series that ran from 1966 to 1969.

63
00:05:42.371 --> 00:05:48.110
Although it wasn't very popular when it was first broadcast, the show was loaded with innovations that would

64
00:05:48.210 --> 00:05:51.472
anticipate and inspire inventions for the next thirty years.

65
00:05:52.332 --> 00:05:56.755
For example, the "communicator" that the lead character, Captain James T.

66
00:05:56.815 --> 00:06:01.138
Kirk, used to speak with his crew was a palm-sized device that flipped open.

67
00:06:01.998 --> 00:06:05.961
Modern cell phones resemble the Star Trek communicator to a startling degree.

68
00:06:06.921 --> 00:06:10.804
Star Trek was also among the first shows to feature talking computers.

69
00:06:11.596 --> 00:06:17.298
The onboard navigation systems in today's luxury cars may not be as complex, but they serve the same

70
00:06:17.378 --> 00:06:19.118
function as the ones on the show.

71
00:06:19.958 --> 00:06:26.940
Years later, Star Trek's popularity skyrocketed when the original cast reunited to make a series of successful movies.

72
00:06:28.221 --> 00:06:32.742
Perhaps Star Trek's greatest contribution was its reflection of changing social norms.

73
00:06:33.742 --> 00:06:38.484
In the future envisioned by Star Trek creator Gene Roddenberry, the world was without racism.

74
00:06:39.344 --> 00:06:45.182
As a result, he gave prominent roles to African-American and Asian actors, among other nationalities.

75
00:06:46.142 --> 00:06:49.463
The African American who played communications officer Lt.

76
00:06:49.543 --> 00:06:55.265
Uhura, Nichelle Nichols, wanted to leave the program after the first season because she was bored with her

77
00:06:55.305 --> 00:07:02.147
role. She stayed because Martin Luther King Jr., the heroic civil rights leader of the 1960s, convinced her

78
00:07:02.607 --> 00:07:06.468
that her character was a revolutionary role model for young African-American women.

79
00:07:07.880 --> 00:07:10.942
Science-fiction writers continue to influence science and society.

80
00:07:12.343 --> 00:07:17.626
The writer Carl Sagan, for example, worked for the United States government in several positions, ranging from a

81
00:07:17.706 --> 00:07:18.966
researcher to a consultant.

82
00:07:20.187 --> 00:07:26.050
It was he who convinced NASA, the American agency in charge of the space program, to include messages

83
00:07:26.190 --> 00:07:29.192
on the two Voyager spacecraft launched in the 1970s.

84
00:07:30.313 --> 00:07:36.256
The messages give information about humans and location of Earth in case the ships are discovered by extraterrestrials.

85
00:07:37.076 --> 00:07:42.219
Few scientists have had the chance to influence the actions of a government quite this much, and some

86
00:07:42.259 --> 00:07:46.001
complain that writers and entertainers should not be advising politicians.

87
00:07:46.962 --> 00:07:52.985
For fans of science fiction, however, the future imagined by its best writers is inspirational, not just for

88
00:07:53.025 --> 00:07:56.487
new technologies, but also for society and human progress.

89
00:07:57.567 --> 00:08:00.169
It's hard to imagine a future without science fiction.
